- How would you optimize the inner max to O(n) using a deque when k is large?
- How would you reconstruct the actual partition that yields the maximum sum?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical graph reasoning: counting shortest paths under a Dijkstra framework, handling path multiplicities without overflow, and edge-case ties.

## Key Concepts

- Dijkstra with path counting
- Tie handling on relaxation
- Complexity and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const N = graph.length;
  const dist = Array(N).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(N).fill(0);
  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({node:s, d:0});
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
     const {node:u, d} = pq.pop();
     if (d !== dist[u]) continue;
     for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {
        const nd = dist[u] + w;
        if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; pq.push({node:v, d:nd}); }
        else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] += ways[u]; }
     }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply modulo to prevent overflow for large counts?
- How would you adapt if edges had zero weight or negative cycles are possible?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of Trie data structures, deduplication strategies, and efficient prefix queries—essential skills for building autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node subtree counts for O(1) prefix counting
- Deduplication via global set to maintain word uniqueness
- Prefix-based retrieval with controlled result limits
- Lexicographic ordering through systematic child traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isWord = false;
    this.passCount = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordSet = new Set();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (this.wordSet.has(word)) return;
    this.wordSet.add(word);
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.passCount++;
    }
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.passCount;
  }

  listWithPrefix(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    const result = [];
    this.dfs(node, prefix, result, limit);
    return result;
  }

  dfs(node, current, result, limit) {
    if (result.length >= limit) return;
    if (node.isWord) {
      result.push(current);
    }
    const sortedKeys = Array.from(node.next.keys()).sort();
    for (const char of sortedKeys) {
      this.dfs(node.next.get(char), current + char, result, limit);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced DP: partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums and both memoized and tabulated solutions. Demonstrates recurrence derivation, base cases, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Time: O(N^2 K); Space: O(NK) (or O(N))
- Edge-case handling for N < K

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, K){
  const N = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<N;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++) dp[i][1]=(pref[i]-pref[0])**2;
  for(let k=2;k<=K;k++){
    for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
      for(let j=k-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + (pref[i]-pref[j])**2);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][K];
}
```

